-- title:
--   Invertible module with section is the zero-scheme ideal's dual
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme and let $M$ be a sheaf of modules over the structure sheaf of $X$. Assume $M$ is invertible in the sense of the predicate `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`: every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ such that the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit sheaf of modules on $U$. Let $s \colon \mathbf 1 \to M$ be a map from the unit object, i.e. a global section of $M$, and write $Z =$ `zeroSchemeIdeal s` for the smallest ideal sheaf datum $J$ on $X$ with $\operatorname{coeffIdeal}(s, U) \le J(U)$ for every affine open $U$, where $\operatorname{coeffIdeal}(s, U)$ is the ideal of $\Gamma(X, U)$ generated by the coefficients of $s$ over $U$ (the infimum is taken over all such $J$). Assume $Z$ is invertible in the sense of the `IdealSheafData` predicate `IsInvertible`: for each $x \in X$ there are an affine open $U$, a section $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x \in X_f$, and a non-zero-divisor $g$ in $\Gamma(X, D(f))$ on the affine basic open $D(f)$ such that $Z(D(f)) = (g)$. The assertion is that there exists an isomorphism $e \colon M \cong Z.\mathrm{invModule}$, where $Z.\mathrm{invModule}$ is the dual $\underline{\mathrm{Hom}}(Z.\mathrm{module}, \mathbf 1)$ of the ideal module $Z.\mathrm{module}$ (the kernel of the map from the unit to the pushforward of the unit along the closed immersion attached to $Z$), such that $s$ followed by $e$ equals $Z.\mathrm{invModuleSection}$, the section obtained by currying the inclusion $Z.\mathrm{moduleι}$.
--
--   This is the classical identification $M \cong \mathcal O_X(Z(s))=\mathcal Hom(\mathcal I_{Z(s)}, \mathcal O_X)$ for an invertible module with a section whose zero scheme is an effective Cartier divisor, matching $s$ with the canonical section $1_{Z(s)}$. It is used in the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors, where a divisor is recovered as the zero scheme of a section of the associated line bundle.
-- source:
